+# Project Memory
+
+`core/memory` keeps the engineering facts an agent needs between sessions, without becoming a second,
+staler copy of the repository. It is the answer to the task's section 6: record what the project *is*, tie
+each fact to when and where it was true, and never let a model's summary stand in for looking at the code.
+
+## Files
+
+| File | Responsibility |
+| --- | --- |
+| `MemoryFact.kt` | `MemoryFact`, `FactSource`, `FactKind`, `MemoryLimits`, retrieval result types |
+| `ProjectMemory.kt` | `record` / `markVerified` / `recall` / `pruneStale` |
+
+Tests: `ProjectMemoryTest` (15).
+
+## The three rules, in code
+
+**1. No secrets.** Every fact is screened with the same `core.security.SecretRedaction` patterns the crash
+reporter uses, plus a private-key header check. A fact whose text contains a credential is *refused*, not
+scrubbed - a partially redacted key is still a leak waiting for the next regex gap. The rejection does not
+say which pattern matched, because that would echo the secret back into the log.
+
+**2. A summary is not the source.** `FactSource.AGENT_SUMMARY` facts are always returned with
+`needsVerification = true` until `markVerified()` is called with the current commit. Facts from
+`SOURCE_CODE`, `BUILD_OUTPUT`, `USER` and `REPO_DOC` are trusted only while both conditions hold:
+
+- their `commitRef` still equals the current head;
+- they were verified within `MemoryLimits.maxAgeMs` (default 14 days).
+
+A fact that fails either check comes back with the reason ("recorded at 414c780, head is 1a8a67a",
+"verified 15 days ago"), so the caller can show it as a hint instead of an answer.
+
+**3. Relevant context only.** `recall(query)` scores facts by keyword overlap with the query over text and
+tags, sorts best-first, and stops at a character budget (4,000 by default). It returns `omittedCount`, so a
+caller can say "3 more facts not shown" instead of silently pretending the memory is complete. `CONSTRAINT`
+facts are always included regardless of the query: the rules an agent must not break are worth the space
+even when the question does not name them.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **`record` refuses conflicting text under the same id.** Re-recording identical text refreshes it;
+  different text under an existing id is an `ID_CONFLICT`, so a decision is never silently overwritten.
+  This is what "update the memory after an important change" has to mean to stay auditable.
+- **`pruneStale` is explicit.** It drops facts from another commit or past the age limit and returns how
+  many went, rather than letting stale facts rot in the store.
+- **Bounded.** 400 facts, 400 characters per fact; the oldest unverified fact is evicted first.
+- **Thread-safe.** `record`/`recall` are called from the task runner and the UI; the test suite writes from
+  eight threads at once.
+
+## What this is not
+
+- Not persisted yet, and not in Git: the rule from the task is that *architectural knowledge people should
+  review* belongs in tracked Markdown (`docs/*.md`, ADRs), while operational facts belong in app storage.
+  This class is the operational half; the tracked half is the documentation you are reading.
+- It does not read the repository by itself; a caller (the future task runner) feeds it facts.
+- It does not decide anything. It hands out facts plus their trust status; `core/policy` and
+  `core/task` decide what may happen.
+
+## Wiring it in (next)
+
+1. On task start, `recall(taskDescription, currentCommit = head, now = now)` and put the result in the
+   agent context - replacing the current "send whatever we remember" behaviour.
+2. After a build or test run, `record` the outcome with `FactSource.BUILD_OUTPUT` and the current commit.
+3. On merge, `pruneStale(newHead)`.
